        "startedAt": "2026-10-18T23:35:31.732Z",
        "claim": {
          "reportedAt": "2026-10-18T23:35:44.089Z",
          "summary": "Root cause: src/money.js percentOf used Math.floor, which truncates percentage amounts instead of rounding to the nearest cent with halves up. For 1999 cents at 15%, 299.85 was truncated to 299, so the net became 1700 instead of 1699 and the 8% tax (135.92 -> 136) left the total at 1836.\n\nFix: percentOf now computes the exact integer product and divides with a round-half-up-towards-+Infinity helper, so discountFor and taxFor (which both use it) follow one documented rule with no floating point drift. invoice.js and the call sites are unchanged; nothing is special-cased.\n\nTests in test/invoice.test.js: reported order returns 1835, discount-only 1699, tax-only 2159, exact-half and just-below-half percentOf cases, and zero percent/zero amount. npm test passes 6/6. Self-check only; independent review still needed."
        },
        "continuations": 0,
